1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,059
Your sides are institutions.

2
00:00:02,959 --> 00:00:05,712
They have stations, and fleets, and work.

3
00:00:07,512 --> 00:00:09,018
Today you write people.

4
00:00:09,918 --> 00:00:12,141
A captain is somebody with a name,

5
00:00:12,591 --> 00:00:14,720
who belongs to one of your sides,

6
00:00:15,170 --> 00:00:18,232
and has an opinion of the crew that's all their own.

7
00:00:20,032 --> 00:00:22,586
The crew can be at war with the Gleaners,

8
00:00:23,036 --> 00:00:25,061
and on good terms with their bookkeeper.

9
00:00:25,961 --> 00:00:27,762
Or the other way round.

10
00:00:28,962 --> 00:00:30,494
A captain is three things.

11
00:00:31,394 --> 00:00:33,678
A name on a button, at a station.

12
00:00:34,128 --> 00:00:37,277
A voice, in your Dialogue chapter, and a memory.

13
00:00:39,077 --> 00:00:41,152
That's all, and I want to be plain about it.

14
00:00:42,052 --> 00:00:43,821
A captain is not a ship.

15
00:00:44,721 --> 00:00:46,806
The game doesn't put one in space for them,

16
00:00:47,256 --> 00:00:48,646
and nobody can shoot at them.

17
00:00:50,446 --> 00:00:52,154
So everything a captain is,

18
00:00:52,604 --> 00:00:56,225
you write in what they say, and what they refuse to say.

19
00:00:57,425 --> 00:01:00,229
A new chapter, and its key is captains.

20
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:04,126
Each captain has four lines.

21
00:01:05,026 --> 00:01:07,030
Side is the side they belong to.

22
00:01:07,930 --> 00:01:10,443
Title follows their name on the button.

23
00:01:11,343 --> 00:01:13,552
Values are what this person cares about,

24
00:01:14,002 --> 00:01:15,907
the same traits as a side has.

25
00:01:16,807 --> 00:01:19,163
And Roams is the system where they're found.

26
00:01:20,963 --> 00:01:22,716
Look at Sable's values.

27
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:25,555
The Gleaners value fear.

28
00:01:26,005 --> 00:01:29,338
She values honesty, and doing things by the book.

29
00:01:30,238 --> 00:01:33,053
She's a bookkeeper among wreckers.

30
00:01:33,503 --> 00:01:35,510
That gap is the character.

31
00:01:36,710 --> 00:01:38,496
One rule about Roams.

32
00:01:39,396 --> 00:01:42,163
The button to hail a captain is on a station.

33
00:01:43,963 --> 00:01:46,662
Any station in that system will do, whoever owns it.

34
00:01:47,562 --> 00:01:50,049
But if the system has no station,

35
00:01:50,499 --> 00:01:52,393
there's nothing to carry the button,

36
00:01:52,843 --> 00:01:54,557
and the captain can't be reached.

37
00:01:56,357 --> 00:01:58,994
So Sable roams the Gleaners' home, which has one.

38
00:01:59,894 --> 00:02:02,694
She does not roam the Bone Pile, which doesn't.

39
00:02:03,594 --> 00:02:05,516
And lint won't warn you about that.

40
00:02:06,716 --> 00:02:09,984
A captain talks the way a side talks, with one difference.

41
00:02:10,884 --> 00:02:13,479
After Speaker, it's the captain's key.

42
00:02:15,279 --> 00:02:18,094
And that changes what one word means.

43
00:02:18,994 --> 00:02:22,648
In this record, standing is the crew's standing with her,

44
00:02:23,098 --> 00:02:24,114
not with her side.

45
00:02:25,014 --> 00:02:27,700
And a deed names her: earns, edda.

46
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:30,626
Now here's something new.

47
00:02:31,526 --> 00:02:34,784
After this answer, the word if, and a comparison.

48
00:02:35,684 --> 00:02:37,197
That's a guard on an answer.

49
00:02:38,097 --> 00:02:40,478
The crew is only offered it when it's true.

50
00:02:42,278 --> 00:02:44,606
So Edda won't gossip with strangers.

51
00:02:45,506 --> 00:02:47,962
Tell her the news once, and she will.

52
00:02:49,162 --> 00:02:54,836
Now for Sable, who has two greetings, and the line between them is minus twenty.

53
00:02:56,636 --> 00:02:58,769
One answer pays her toll,

54
00:02:59,219 --> 00:03:01,822
and earns both of the things she values.

55
00:03:02,722 --> 00:03:03,892
The other insults her,

56
00:03:04,342 --> 00:03:07,489
and it names the far end of each of those traits.

57
00:03:08,389 --> 00:03:10,691
Once is enough to cross the line.

58
00:03:12,491 --> 00:03:15,108
And the answer that matters, the one about the Tern,

59
00:03:15,558 --> 00:03:17,480
is only for a crew she trusts.

60
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:22,223
That's a rival, and that's all a rival is.

61
00:03:23,123 --> 00:03:24,393
A line you chose,

62
00:03:24,843 --> 00:03:27,248
and what you wrote on each side of it.

63
00:03:28,448 --> 00:03:31,803
If you read the Open Universe's own files,

64
00:03:32,253 --> 00:03:34,694
you'll see a line called Rival when.

65
00:03:36,494 --> 00:03:38,365
It looks like exactly what we want.

66
00:03:39,265 --> 00:03:42,591
Lint accepts it, and today, the game doesn't read it.

67
00:03:43,491 --> 00:03:45,533
I tried it, and nothing changed.

68
00:03:47,333 --> 00:03:48,748
So don't rely on it.

69
00:03:49,648 --> 00:03:54,569
Make your rival out of guards, the way we just did.

70
00:03:56,369 --> 00:03:58,533
I save, and lint says clean.

71
00:03:59,433 --> 00:04:02,864
And as usual, the list on the page is the rest of the check.

72
00:04:04,064 --> 00:04:06,272
At home, the station has a new button,

73
00:04:06,722 --> 00:04:08,894
with Edda's name and title on it.

74
00:04:10,694 --> 00:04:14,017
She's short with me, and I've got two answers.

75
00:04:14,917 --> 00:04:16,073
I tell her the news.

76
00:04:17,873 --> 00:04:20,560
I hail her again, and it's a different greeting,

77
00:04:21,010 --> 00:04:22,270
and there's a third answer.

78
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:26,001
She tells me where to find Sable,

79
00:04:26,451 --> 00:04:28,166
and to settle up first.

80
00:04:29,366 --> 00:04:32,038
This is Gleaner space, and we're at war with them.

81
00:04:32,938 --> 00:04:34,578
And here's Sable, anyway.

82
00:04:36,378 --> 00:04:37,889
I pay her toll.

83
00:04:38,789 --> 00:04:41,083
Now she'll answer the question about the Tern.

84
00:04:42,883 --> 00:04:43,911
So let's throw that away.

85
00:04:44,811 --> 00:04:47,118
I tell her to bill the Compact, twice.

86
00:04:48,918 --> 00:04:49,999
And listen to her now.

87
00:04:50,899 --> 00:04:52,287
The question's gone, too.

88
00:04:54,087 --> 00:04:55,509
Nothing else has changed.

89
00:04:55,959 --> 00:04:57,329
Nobody is out hunting me.

90
00:04:58,229 --> 00:05:00,173
A rival greets you differently,

91
00:05:00,623 --> 00:05:02,528
and keeps back what a friend would hear.

92
00:05:03,428 --> 00:05:05,192
Write that well, and it's plenty.

93
00:05:06,392 --> 00:05:07,394
Now it's your turn.

94
00:05:08,294 --> 00:05:10,222
Write one captain for each side,

95
00:05:10,672 --> 00:05:13,751
and give each a value their side doesn't have.

96
00:05:14,651 --> 00:05:16,897
Two greetings, three answers.

97
00:05:17,347 --> 00:05:20,465
One earns, one costs, and one is only for a friend.

98
00:05:22,265 --> 00:05:25,089
Then decide whether the crew can win a rival back.

99
00:05:26,889 --> 00:05:28,553
Next time, we tidy up.

100
00:05:29,003 --> 00:05:32,322
Your file gets split into chapters, and you print the world.
